/* Symbols a `--jit` KERNEL resolves against the rakupp executable that
 * dlopen'd it. See docs/dev/plans/JIT-PLAN.md and src/Jit.cpp.
 *
 * ELF only, and a separate file from rakupp_ext.dynlist on purpose. That one
 * is the extension ABI: a promise to code outside this tree, kept narrow
 * because breaking it breaks someone else's build. This one is a promise to
 * nobody. A kernel is compiled by the SAME build of rakupp that loads it, from
 * the same headers, and dies with the process — so what it binds to is an
 * internal detail that may change in any commit.
 *
 * Without this, `--jit` cannot work on ELF at all: a plain ELF executable
 * keeps its symbols out of .dynsym, so a kernel's first call to
 * rakupp::applyArith fails at dlopen with "undefined symbol". Mach-O exports
 * its globals by default, which is why the backend ran on macOS from the start
 * and had never once entered a kernel on Linux.
 *
 * Why a glob over the namespace, rather than the three names a kernel needs
 * today (rakupp::applyArith, Interpreter::boolify, Value::truthy): a kernel
 * calls whatever the codegen emits, that codegen is shared with `--exe`, and
 * an enumerated list would go stale in silence. The miss would surface only on
 * ELF, only at dlopen, and only for the loop shape that reached the new call —
 * which is exactly the shape of the bug this file exists to fix. The glob
 * costs about a thousand .dynsym entries and cannot go stale.
 *
 * This is still not -rdynamic, which would also export the statically linked
 * libstdc++ and everything else this binary absorbs.
 *
 * _ZN  a name inside a namespace        _ZTV  vtable
 * _ZNK a const member function          _ZTI  typeinfo
 *                                       _ZTS  typeinfo name
 * The last three are what an exception thrown through a kernel needs to find
 * its handler back in the interpreter. Understood by GNU ld, gold and lld.
 */
{
  _ZN6rakupp*;
  _ZNK6rakupp*;
  _ZTVN6rakupp*;
  _ZTIN6rakupp*;
  _ZTSN6rakupp*;
};
